# Cloudflare Workers Assets reads this file the same way Cloudflare Pages
# does, applying these headers to matching static-asset responses (2026-08-31,
# found via a real PageSpeed "use efficient cache lifetimes" audit): every
# static asset served through the Worker's ASSETS binding — the local
# celebrity photography, hero images, even the content-hashed /_next/static
# build output — was coming back `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` (confirmed directly against the live deployment), i.e. no
# real caching at all, with nothing in this project's own code able to touch
# it (these responses never reach the Worker's own JS).
#
# /_next/static/* is safe to cache forever: webpack content-hashes every file
# in that path, so a new build always gets new URLs, never a stale one under
# an old URL. Everything else here (celebrity/, hero/, logos) is NOT
# content-hashed — the filenames stay stable across a real re-upload (see
# scripts/generate-celebrities.mjs's own comment) — so those get a real but
# finite lifetime instead of `immutable`, long enough to matter for a repeat
# visitor without risking a long-stuck-stale asset after the next update.

/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/celebrity/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/hero/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/logo.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/logo-mark.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/footer-chain.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/placeholder-product.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
